one small college town is taking a  collective sigh of relief another is now  in the spotlight for its connection to  an alleged serial killer Brian coburger  is accused of stabbing four University  of Idaho students to death inside of  their off-campus home coburger was on  the path toward a doctorate at nearby  Washington State University and students  in Pullman Washington began their spring  semester on Monday in fact many told  reporters that they had never felt  unsafe in their in their town before now  professors took part in trauma workshops  to prepare for any stress that students  might be dealing with people who live  and work a few miles away in Moscow  Idaho say that they're proud of their  Police Department's response to the to  the rare crime  well I think they got a lot of flack for  uh keeping their cards tight to their  chest which I always felt like that's  what they were doing and um so I was  pretty elated when  the when they caught this guy and um all  this evidence was revealed and I felt  like they needed to be thanked it's been  a big deal I mean I'm a little emotional  to talk about it I'm  you know the students here are family to  us they're our customers they're our  friends and um it was such a tragic  horrible thing that happened that it  affected us all very deeply as for  coburger his defense team is expected  back in court for a status hearing on  Thursday after seeing the apparent  mountain of evidence against coburger  and the probable cause affidavit we  asked celebrity defense attorney Alan  dershowitz how would he handle a case  like this  every case is different and every  defense is different I am always opposed  to lawyers who say I always do it this  way I always put the client on the stand  I never put the client on the stand  remember too the affidavit only presents  one side of the story uh it's only the  prosecution's version you have to begin  to develop your own evidence and I'm  going to surprise people when I say that  a good criminal defense lawyer always  starts with a presumption of guilt not a  presumption of innocence you always  assume your client is guilty and then  you work back from there if you assume  your client is innocent you'll make  mistakes you'll allow searches you'll  allow him to speak to the police so you  always start with a presumption of guilt  and then try to develop evidence that  questions or challenges  Brian you're the resident defense  attorney you agree with Mr dershowitz  would you have a similar game plan  presumption of guilt I guess according  to dershowitz I'm not a good defense  attorney every defense attorney does it  differently I don't buy into that I  think I maybe do something a little  similar I go with the presumption of  innocence but the prosecution's case is  the strongest thing against him and I  think if you look at it that way we're  saying hey I believe my guy is innocent  and I'm just talking about cases where I  know I got to go to trial I'm not  talking about negotiating please but if  it's a trial case I go with the  Assumption of my client is Innocent but  the prosecution has the strongest case  in the world if you're going with that  mindset you fight a little bit harder to  try to disprove their case of course if  you can't disprove their case then  you're looking to try to negotiate but  in a case like this if you presume that  your client is guilty I don't think  they're going to be working as hard but  I understand what he's saying you got to  really attack the prosecution's case  thinking that it is stronger than  anything else that you can see  so Terry let's assume the worst for  Coburg let's say his defense strategy  loses it doesn't work the jury then is  going to maybe have to decide the issue  of the death penalty if it's pursued by  prosecutors you think a jury would  impose the death penalty in this kind of  case you know that's a really tough  question we know that the prosecution  has asked for the death penalty but that  doesn't mean that they're going to have  a jury that's going to actually execute  on that so basically you're going to  have to make sure you are selecting  jurors who don't have any sort of  opposition to the death penalty and it  obviously has to be unanimous they're  going to go through aggravating  circumstances they're going to go  through mitigating circumstances and try  to determine whether or not one  outweighs the other and if you get just  one juror who doesn't agree you're not  going to get the death penalty and we've  seen this again I'm mentioning the  Parkland shooter case we absolutely  thought many people thought that that  was going to be a death penalty case and  at the end of the day the jury didn't  have a unanimous decision as far as that  was concerned so we got life in prison  there and something like that could  happen here even if there is a  conviction it's a heinous crime but  there's no guarantee that an entire jury  is going to agree to that yeah you can  somehow sometimes predict you know how a  jury is going to weigh in terms of guilt  or not guilty but death penalty I agree  with you sometimes so unpredictable  Brian Terry thanks so much great work  we're going to see you next time  everybody as we discuss Justice in  America
 

